 </nav>

 <section id="tahajjud-how-to-pray" aria-labelledby="tahajjud-how-title" class="tahajjud-guide-section">
  <h3 id="tahajjud-how-title">How to pray Tahajjud, step by step</h3>
  <p>You do not need a special sequence of new prayer movements. Tahajjud follows the familiar form of salah. If you are learning to pray for the first time, start with the foundations of the obligatory prayers.</p>
  <ol class="tahajjud-steps">
   <li><strong>Pray Isha first.</strong> Night prayer is offered after Isha and before the beginning of Fajr. Many people sleep and then wake to pray; the term <em>Tahajjud</em> is often used particularly for prayer after sleep, while <em>qiyam al-layl</em> is the wider term for night worship.</li>
   <li><strong>Choose a manageable time.</strong> Use the calculator's last-third start if you hope to wake late in the night. If that is difficult, pray earlier in the night rather than abandoning voluntary worship altogether.</li>
   <li><strong>Prepare for salah.</strong> Make wudu if needed, wear appropriate clothing, face the Qibla and make your intention in your heart. There is no required spoken intention formula for this guide.</li>
   <li><strong>Begin the first rak'ah.</strong> Say the opening takbir, recite Surah al-Fatihah and then Quran you know. Complete ruku and sujud as in your usual prayer, then stand for the second rak'ah.</li>
   <li><strong>Complete the second rak'ah.</strong> Recite al-Fatihah and Quran, complete the prayer's movements, sit for the final tashahhud and end with salam according to the prayer method you follow.</li>
   <li><strong>Continue if you wish.</strong> You may pray another pair of rak'ahs. The Prophet ﷺ described voluntary night prayer in pairs. You do not have to complete a long session to begin. <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1137" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1137</a>.</li>
   <li><strong>Make dua and seek forgiveness.</strong> Speak sincerely to Allah about your needs, offer thanks, ask for guidance and forgiveness. You can use the Quranic supplications below. Outside the formal prayer you can make personal dua in your own words.</li>
   <li><strong>Consider Witr before Fajr.</strong> If you have not yet prayed Witr, arrange it according to your school and circumstances. The number and method of Witr differ between schools; do not assume every Muslim prays Witr in the same way.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="tahajjud-source-line">The two-by-two form of night prayer is described in <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1137" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1137</a>; the Prophet’s practice of sleeping and then rising for prayer is reported in <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1146" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1146</a>.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="tahajjud-guide-section" id="tahajjud-rakats" aria-labelledby="tahajjud-rakat-title">
  <h3 id="tahajjud-rakat-title">How many rak'ahs? And what about Witr?</h3>
  <p><strong>Start with two rak'ahs</strong> if you are new to night prayer. You can add further pairs when able. Reports describe different details of the Prophet's night prayers, including an account of eleven rak'ahs inclusive of Witr; that report should not be treated as a universal mandatory count for everyone. <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1147" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1147</a>.</p>
  <div class="tahajjud-witr-notes">
   <div>
    <h4>If you expect to wake up</h4>
    <p>You may plan Witr for the end of your night worship, before Fajr, following your school of practice. <a href="https://sunnah.com/muslim:755b" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih Muslim 755b</a>.</p>
   </div>
   <div>
    <h4>If you might not wake up</h4>
    <p>You may pray Witr before sleeping rather than risk missing it. If you later wake and offer more voluntary prayer, do not perform a second Witr that night. <a href="https://sunnah.com/abudawud:1439" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sunan Abi Dawud 1439</a>.</p>
   </div>
  </div>
  <p class="tahajjud-caution">The legal classification, number of rak'ahs and performance of Witr differ among schools (including the Hanafi school). For a specific ruling, follow your trusted teacher or local scholarly guidance.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="tahajjud-guide-section" id="tahajjud-duas" aria-labelledby="tahajjud-dua-title">
  <h3 id="tahajjud-dua-title">Duas you can make during the night</h3>
  <p>You are not required to recite a particular “Tahajjud-only dua”. A hadith highlights supplication and forgiveness in the last third of the night (<a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1145" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1145</a>). These Quranic phrases are meaningful examples to learn, reflect on and use in personal supplication.</p>
  <p class="tahajjud-source-line">The simple transliterations below are pronunciation aids, not replacements for learning accurate Arabic recitation. English explanations are original summaries of meaning; follow the Quran references for context. Rules for supplication <em>within</em> formal salah vary by school; personal supplication after finishing prayer is a straightforward starting point.</p>
  <div class="tahajjud-dua-grid">
   {duas.map((dua)=>
    <article class="tahajjud-dua" aria-label={dua.title}>
     <h4>{dua.title}</h4>
     <p class="tahajjud-arabic" lang="ar" dir="rtl">{dua.arabic}</p>
     <p class="tahajjud-transliteration"><strong>Transliteration:</strong> {dua.transliteration}</p>
     <p class="tahajjud-meaning"><strong>Meaning:</strong> {dua.meaning}</p>
     <p class="tahajjud-reference"><a href={dua.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read {dua.reference} in context</a></p>
    </article>
   )}
  </div>
  <aside class="tahajjud-waking" aria-label="Supplication on waking">
   <h4>A short supplication for forgiveness</h4>
   <p class="tahajjud-arabic" lang="ar" dir="rtl">اللَّهُمَّ اغْفِرْ لِي</p>
   <p><strong>Allahumma-ghfir li</strong> — ask Allah to forgive you.</p>
   <p>The words are found within a longer remembrance narrated in <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1154" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1154</a> about waking in the night. Read the full narration to learn its context; this short phrase alone is not presented as the complete narrated formula.</p>
  </aside>
 </section>

 <section class="tahajjud-guide-section" id="tahajjud-beginners" aria-labelledby="tahajjud-beginner-title">
  <h3 id="tahajjud-beginner-title">Questions and tips for beginners</h3>
  <div class="tahajjud-practice-faq">
   <details open><summary>Is Tahajjud obligatory?</summary><p>No. It is voluntary worship. Keep the obligatory prayers as your priority. The excellence of voluntary night prayer is described in <a href="https://sunnah.com/muslim:1163a" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih Muslim 1163a</a>.</p></details>
   <details><summary>Do I have to sleep before Tahajjud?</summary><p>Scholars often use <em>Tahajjud</em> especially for night prayer after sleeping; the wider category of night prayer, <em>qiyam al-layl</em>, is not restricted to people who wake after sleeping. You may still offer voluntary night prayer after Isha if you cannot wake later.</p></details>
   <details><summary>Is there a fixed Surah to read in each rak'ah?</summary><p>No single Surah is required specifically for all Tahajjud prayers. Recite al-Fatihah and further Quran you know, taking the time you can manage. Do not assume you need to memorise particular lengthy Surahs to start.</p></details>
   <details><summary>What if the last third starts after midnight?</summary><p>That is normal. The last third is calculated from Maghrib to the next Fajr, not from the civil midnight clock. For example, an 18:00 Maghrib and 05:00 Fajr gives a last-third start of about 01:20.</p></details>
   <details><summary>Can I pray after Witr?</summary><p>Yes, further voluntary night prayer can be offered after Witr according to widely followed guidance. Do not repeat Witr in the same night. Details of timing and Witr practice differ between schools, so refer to your trusted local authority. <a href="https://sunnah.com/abudawud:1439" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sunan Abi Dawud 1439</a>.</p></details>
   <details><summary>What if I only have ten minutes?</summary><p>Begin with two attentive rak'ahs, then make sincere dua and seek forgiveness. Regular, manageable worship is better than setting an unrealistic target and giving up. Leave enough time for Fajr.</p></details>
  </div>
 </section>

 <section id="tahajjud-references" class="tahajjud-guide-section tahajjud-references" aria-labelledby="tahajjud-reference-title">
  <h3 id="tahajjud-reference-title">Quran and hadith references</h3>
  <p>These primary references explain night prayer, its timing and the place of supplication. They are linked directly so you can read the full context.</p>
  <ul>
   <li><a href="https://quran.com/17/79" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 17:79</a> — night worship.</li>
   <li><a href="https://quran.com/51/17-18" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 51:17–18</a> — worship at night and asking forgiveness before dawn.</li>
   <li><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1137" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1137</a> — night prayer in pairs.</li>
   <li><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1145" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1145</a> — seeking, asking and forgiveness in the last third.</li>
   <li><a href="https://sunnah.com/muslim:755b" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih Muslim 755b</a> — Witr early or late depending on whether one expects to wake.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="tahajjud-caution">This is a general learning guide; prayer details and Witr practice differ among scholarly schools. It does not replace guidance from a qualified teacher for your personal circumstances.</p>
 </section>
